= Solution
{parent=i}

The <total derivative> of $f$ at $x$ is a <linear map> $Df(x):\mathbb R^n\to\mathbb R$ satisfying
$$
\boxed{\lim_{h\to0}\frac{|f(x+h)-f(x)-Df(x)h|}{\|h\|}=0.}
$$
The <gradient> is the <vector> of <partial derivatives>, $\nabla f(x)=(\partial_1f(x),\ldots,\partial_nf(x))$. When $f$ is totally differentiable, these <partial derivatives> exist and
$$
\boxed{Df(x)h=\nabla f(x)\cdot h=\sum_{j=1}^n\partial_jf(x)h_j.}
$$
Thus the derivative is a linear functional represented by the <gradient> under the <Euclidean inner product>. Existence of <partial derivatives> alone does not imply total differentiability.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The denominator is positive on $B$ because $x_3>-1$. The <quotient rule> gives the <gradient>
$$
\boxed{\nabla g(x)=\left(\frac1{x_3+3},\ \frac1{x_3+3},\ -\frac{x_1+x_2}{(x_3+3)^2}\right).}
$$
In particular $g$ is continuously differentiable throughout $B$.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The scalar-valued multivariable <mean value theorem> states that, if $f$ is differentiable on an <open set> containing the closed line segment from $x$ to $y$, then for $x\ne y$ there exists $s\in(0,1)$ such that
$$
\boxed{f(y)-f(x)=Df(x+s(y-x))(y-x)=\nabla f(x+s(y-x))\cdot(y-x).}
$$
This follows by applying the one-dimensional <mean value theorem> to $t\mapsto f(x+t(y-x))$ on $[0,1]$. The segment condition is necessary for this formulation on a possibly nonconvex domain.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For every $z\in B$, $z_3+3>2$ and $|z_1+z_2|<2$. Consequently each of the three <partial derivatives> in the preceding <gradient> has absolute value below $1/2$, so
$$
\|\nabla g(z)\|^2<\frac14+\frac14+\frac14=\frac34.
$$
The segment joining $x,y\in B$ lies inside $B$. The <mean value theorem> and <Cauchy-Schwarz inequality> therefore give
$$
|g(y)-g(x)|\leq\|\nabla g(x+s(y-x))\|\,\|y-x\|\leq\boxed{\frac{\sqrt3}{2}\|y-x\|}.
$$
For $x=y$ both sides vanish. This proves the required <Lipschitz continuity> uniformly on the ball.

= c
{parent=1}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

A $C^1$ <diffeomorphism> $F:U\to V$ is a <bijection> for which both $F$ and $F^{-1}$ are continuously differentiable. If a higher regularity class is specified, both maps must belong to that class. In particular, a <diffeomorphism> is a <homeomorphism>, and the <chain rule> gives $D(F^{-1})(F(x))=(DF(x))^{-1}$.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <inverse function theorem> states: let $F:U\subseteq\mathbb R^n\to\mathbb R^n$ be $C^1$, with $U$ open, and suppose $DF(x_0)$ is invertible. Then there exist open neighbourhoods $U_0\subseteq U$ of $x_0$ and $V_0$ of $F(x_0)$ such that $F:U_0\to V_0$ is a $C^1$ <diffeomorphism>. Its inverse derivative satisfies
$$
\boxed{D(F^{-1})(F(x))=(DF(x))^{-1},\qquad x\in U_0.}
$$
The conclusion is local; invertibility of one <Jacobian matrix> does not assert global injectivity.

= iii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The map $G$ is $C^1$ on the open ball $B$. Its <Jacobian matrix> is
$$
DG(x)=\begin{pmatrix}(x_3+3)^{-1}&(x_3+3)^{-1}&-(x_1+x_2)(x_3+3)^{-2}\\0&1&0\\0&0&1\end{pmatrix}.
$$
At the origin $G(0)=0$ and $\boxed{\det DG(0)=1/3\ne0}$. The <inverse function theorem> therefore supplies the required <open sets> $V_1\subset B$ and $V_2\subset\mathbb R^3$, both containing zero, with $G:V_1\to V_2$ a <diffeomorphism>. The explicit inverse formula on its image,
$$
G^{-1}(y_1,y_2,y_3)=(y_1(y_3+3)-y_2,\ y_2,\ y_3),
$$
also checks the local conclusion directly.

= G
{parent=2013}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Calculus of Variations}

= 1
{parent=G}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=1}

Take independent smooth <variations> $y+\varepsilon\eta$ and $z+\varepsilon\zeta$. Differentiating the <functional> and using <integration by parts> gives its <first variation>
$$
\delta J=[F_{y'}\eta+F_{z'}\zeta]_a^b+\int_a^b\left[(F_y-\tfrac d{dx}F_{y'})\eta+(F_z-\tfrac d{dx}F_{z'})\zeta\right]dx.
$$
First choose <variations> vanishing at the endpoints. To use the stated form of the <fundamental lemma of the calculus of variations>, take $\eta=(x-a)(b-x)\chi$ with arbitrary smooth $\chi$, and similarly for $\zeta$. The lemma makes $(x-a)(b-x)(F_y-dF_{y'}/dx)$ vanish, and hence makes the unweighted coefficient vanish in the interior; continuity extends this to the endpoints. Applying the same argument to $z$ gives the <Euler-Lagrange equations>
$$
\boxed{\frac d{dx}F_{y'}=F_y,\qquad\frac d{dx}F_{z'}=F_z.}
$$
If either endpoint value of $y$ or $z$ is free, the corresponding variation there is arbitrary. The remaining boundary term gives the <natural boundary conditions for a free endpoint>: $F_{y'}=0$ for a free $y$ value and $F_{z'}=0$ for a free $z$ value at that endpoint. With both coordinates free at both endpoints, all four conditions apply.

For the specified integrand these equations reduce to
$$
y''+5y-2z=0,\qquad z''+2y=0,
$$
and the natural conditions are $y'=z'=0$ at each free endpoint. Eliminating $z$ gives
$$
y^{(4)}+5y''+4y=0,\qquad (D^2+1)(D^2+4)y=0.
$$
Its four independent modes give the full general solution
$$
\begin{aligned}
y&=A\cos x+C\sin x+B\cos2x+D\sin2x,\\
z&=2A\cos x+2C\sin x+\tfrac12B\cos2x+\tfrac12D\sin2x.
\end{aligned}
$$
At zero, $y'(0)=C+2D$ and $z'(0)=2C+D$. Vanishing forces $C=D=0$, giving
$$
\boxed{y=A\cos x+B\cos2x,\qquad z=2A\cos x+\frac B2\cos2x.}
$$
At $x=\pi/6$ the two derivative conditions become $A/2+\sqrt3B=0$ and $A+\sqrt3B/2=0$. Their coefficient determinant is nonzero, so $A=B=0$. Thus **the unique stationary extremal with all four natural boundary conditions is $y=z=0$**. The word extremal here means a stationary point of the functional; the indefinite integrand does not require this point to be a minimum.

= H
{parent=2013}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Classical Mechanics}

= 1
{parent=H}
{scope}

= a
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Let $s$ be distance along the rod from its fixed end. The position of that material point is $s\,n(t)$, where $n=(\sin\theta\cos\phi,\sin\theta\sin\phi,-\cos\theta)$ is a <unit vector>. Its <velocity> is $s\dot n$, and direct differentiation gives
$$
|\dot n|^2=\dot\theta^2+\sin^2\theta\,\dot\phi^2.
$$
The uniform linear <mass density> is $M/a$. Integrating the elemental <kinetic energy> therefore proves, rather than assumes, the rod formula:
$$
T=\frac12\int_0^a s^2|\dot n|^2\frac Ma\,ds=\boxed{\frac{Ma^2}{6}(\dot\theta^2+\sin^2\theta\,\dot\phi^2).}
$$
Equivalently, its <moment of inertia> about a perpendicular axis through the pivot is $Ma^2/3$.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <centre of mass> is at height $-a\cos\theta/2$. With gravitational <potential energy> zero at the pivot level,
$$
V=-\frac{Mga}{2}\cos\theta.
$$
Hence the <Lagrangian> is
$$
\boxed{L=T-V=\frac{Ma^2}{6}(\dot\theta^2+\sin^2\theta\,\dot\phi^2)+\frac{Mga}{2}\cos\theta.}
$$
Adding a constant to the potential would change the numerical <energy> reference but would not change the motion.

= c
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For an autonomous <Lagrangian>, the <Euler-Lagrange equations> imply
$$
\frac d{dt}\left(\sum_{q=\theta,\phi}\dot q\frac{\partial L}{\partial\dot q}-L\right)=-\frac{\partial L}{\partial t}=0.
$$
Indeed, differentiating the expression cancels the acceleration terms, leaving $\sum_q\dot q(dL_{\dot q}/dt-L_q)-L_t$, which vanishes. Here $T$ is quadratic in the velocities, so $\sum\dot qL_{\dot q}=2T$. Thus the conserved <energy> is
$$
\boxed{H=T+V=E.}
$$
The azimuth $\phi$ is a <cyclic coordinate>. Its conjugate <momentum> is constant:
$$
p_\phi=\frac{\partial L}{\partial\dot\phi}=\frac{Ma^2}{3}\sin^2\theta\,\dot\phi.
$$
Therefore $\boxed{\sin^2\theta\,\dot\phi=h}$ is constant as well. Physically, $p_\phi$ is the vertical component of <angular momentum>.

= d
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Away from the coordinate poles, use $\dot\phi=h/\sin^2\theta$ in the conserved <energy>:
$$
E=\frac{Ma^2}{6}\dot\theta^2+\frac{Ma^2h^2}{6\sin^2\theta}-\frac{Mga}{2}\cos\theta.
$$
Rearranging gives the first-order equation
$$
\boxed{\dot\theta^2=\frac{6E}{Ma^2}+\frac{3g}{a}\cos\theta-\frac{h^2}{\sin^2\theta}.}
$$
Where the right side is positive, either sign of its square root is possible, according to the direction of motion. Its zeros are turning points. For $h\ne0$, the centrifugal term excludes the poles; if $h=0$, the polar-coordinate formula is interpreted through the original rod equations.

= e
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

The polar <Euler-Lagrange equation> is
$$
\ddot\theta=\sin\theta\cos\theta\,\dot\phi^2-\frac{3g}{2a}\sin\theta.
$$
For a constant angle with $0<\theta_0<\pi$, conservation of $h$ makes $\dot\phi$ constant, say $\Omega$. The remaining equation is
$$
\cos\theta_0\,\Omega^2=\frac{3g}{2a}.
$$
If $g>0$, every nonvertical constant-angle motion is a <conical pendulum> motion:
$$
\boxed{0<\theta_0<\frac\pi2,\qquad\phi(t)=\phi_0\pm\sqrt{\frac{3g}{2a\cos\theta_0}}\,t.}
$$
The rod sweeps out a cone about the downward vertical, in either azimuthal direction. Equivalently, $h^2=(3g/(2a))\sin^4\theta_0/\cos\theta_0$. The exactly downward and exactly upward vertical rods are also static solutions; azimuth has no physical meaning at either pole. No static tilted rod is possible under nonzero gravity.

If $g=0$, the condition is $\sin\theta_0\cos\theta_0\Omega^2=0$. Thus **a rod may remain at rest in any orientation, or rotate uniformly in the horizontal plane** with $\theta_0=\pi/2$ and arbitrary constant $\Omega$. The vertical cases are included among static orientations; changing only their azimuth does not move the rod.

