= Solution
{parent=a}

Put $S=\sum_{j=1}^n|X_j|$. The <log-likelihood> for the <Laplace distribution> is
$$
\ell(\theta)=-n\log(2\theta)-S/\theta,\qquad\ell'(\theta)=-n/\theta+S/\theta^2.
$$
For $S>0$ the derivative is positive for $\theta<S/n$ and negative for $\theta>S/n$, so the unique <maximum likelihood estimator> is
$$
\boxed{\widehat\theta=\frac1n\sum_{j=1}^n|X_j|.}
$$
The exceptional data set $S=0$ has <probability> zero; there the likelihood has its supremum at the excluded boundary $\theta=0$. For the <Fisher information>, $|X_j|$ has exponential density $\theta^{-1}e^{-y/\theta}$ for $y>0$, hence mean $\theta$. Since $\ell''(\theta)=n/\theta^2-2S/\theta^3$,
$$
\boxed{I_n(\theta)=-\mathbb E_\theta\ell''(\theta)=\frac n{\theta^2}.}
$$
This also follows from the score <variance>, because $\operatorname{Var}(S)=n\theta^2$.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The absolute observations are independent <exponential random variables> with mean $\theta$ and <variance> $\theta^2$. Applying the <central limit theorem> directly to their average proves
$$
\boxed{\frac{\sqrt n(\widehat\theta-\theta)}{\theta}\ \xrightarrow{d}\ N(0,1).}
$$
Thus the large-sample approximation is $\widehat\theta\approx N(\theta,\theta^2/n)$, consistent with inverse <Fisher information>. Also $\widehat\theta\to\theta$ in <probability> by the <weak law of large numbers>, so $\theta$ in the standard error can be replaced by $\widehat\theta$ through <Slutsky's theorem>.

= c
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Let $z=z_{1-\alpha/2}=\Phi^{-1}(1-\alpha/2)$, where $0<\alpha<1$. By the preceding <asymptotic normality> and <Slutsky's theorem>, $\sqrt n(\widehat\theta-\theta)/\widehat\theta$ is approximately standard normal. Inverting $|\sqrt n(\widehat\theta-\theta)/\widehat\theta|\leq z$ gives the <Wald confidence interval>
$$
\boxed{\left[\widehat\theta-z\frac{\widehat\theta}{\sqrt n},\quad\widehat\theta+z\frac{\widehat\theta}{\sqrt n}\right]\cap(0,\infty).}
$$
Its coverage approaches $1-\alpha$ as $n\to\infty$. The interpretation of the <confidence interval> is repeated-sample coverage of the fixed parameter; it does not assign a posterior <probability> to $\theta$.

= 2
{parent=O}
