= Solution
{parent=a}

Let $s=\sum_{j=1}^nx_j$. The <Bernoulli distribution> likelihood is proportional to $\theta^s(1-\theta)^{n-s}$. Multiplication by the <beta distribution> prior gives
$$
\pi(\theta\mid x)\propto\theta^{a+s-1}(1-\theta)^{b+n-s-1}.
$$
Normalizing with the <beta function> therefore gives
$$
\boxed{\theta\mid x\sim\operatorname{Beta}(a+s,b+n-s),\qquad\pi(\theta\mid x)=\frac{\theta^{a+s-1}(1-\theta)^{b+n-s-1}}{B(a+s,b+n-s)}.}
$$
The parameters remain positive. This is <beta-binomial conjugacy>: successes add to the first shape parameter and failures to the second.

= b
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

A $100(1-\alpha)\%$ <credible set> is a measurable subset $C(x)\subseteq(0,1)$ with posterior <probability> $1-\alpha$:
$$
\boxed{\int_{C(x)}\pi(\theta\mid x)\,d\theta=1-\alpha.}
$$
In ordinary language, **given the observed data and the chosen prior model, the <probability> that the parameter lies in this set is $1-\alpha$**. Such a set need not be an interval or be unique; its defining requirement is posterior mass.

= c
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Let $F_{A,B}$ denote the distribution function of the posterior <beta distribution>, with $A=a+s$ and $B=b+n-s$. This distribution is continuous with positive density on $(0,1)$. Its <equal-tailed credible interval> is
$$
\boxed{\left[F_{A,B}^{-1}(\alpha/2),\quad F_{A,B}^{-1}(1-\alpha/2)\right].}
$$
Compute the two quantiles by solving $\int_0^{\theta_L}\pi(\theta\mid x)d\theta=\alpha/2$ and $\int_{\theta_U}^1\pi(\theta\mid x)d\theta=\alpha/2$. Each excluded tail has mass $\alpha/2$, leaving posterior mass $1-\alpha$ between the endpoints.

= d
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

The further observation is independent of the earlier observations conditional on $\theta$. Integrate its conditional success <probability> against the posterior:
$$
\mathbb P(X_{n+1}=1\mid x)=\int_0^1\theta\,\pi(\theta\mid x)d\theta=\frac{B(A+1,B)}{B(A,B)}.
$$
The given factorial formula for the <beta function> gives this ratio as $A/(A+B)$. Thus the <posterior predictive probability> is
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(X_{n+1}=1\mid x)=\frac{a+s}{a+b+n}.}
$$
It is the posterior mean of the Bernoulli success parameter. Independence here is conditional: sharing the uncertain parameter generally makes past and future observations dependent before conditioning on it.

