= Solution
{parent=a}

The <Lagrange interpolation polynomial> is
$$
\boxed{p(x)=\sum_{j=0}^nf(x_j)\ell_j(x),\qquad\ell_j(x)=\prod_{\substack{0\leq k\leq n\\k\ne j}}\frac{x-x_k}{x_j-x_k}.}
$$
The distinct nodes make all denominators nonzero. Each <Lagrange basis polynomial> has degree $n$ and satisfies $\ell_j(x_i)=\delta_{ij}$, so the sum has degree at most $n$ and interpolates every specified value.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

If $p$ and $q$ both interpolate the data and have degree at most $n$, the <polynomial> $r=p-q$ has the $n+1$ distinct <roots> $x_0,\ldots,x_n$. A nonzero <polynomial> of degree at most $n$ has at most $n$ roots: repeated use of the <factor theorem> would otherwise make a product of $n+1$ linear factors divide it. Hence $r=0$, proving **the interpolating <polynomial> is unique**.

= c
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Integrate the <Lagrange interpolation polynomial> and exchange the <integral> with the finite sum:
$$
\int_a^bp(x)dx=\sum_{j=0}^nf(x_j)\int_a^b\ell_j(x)dx.
$$
Therefore the <interpolatory quadrature> weights are
$$
\boxed{w_j=\int_a^b\ell_j(x)dx=\int_a^b\prod_{k\ne j}\frac{x-x_k}{x_j-x_k}\,dx.}
$$
The resulting <quadrature rule> is exact for every <polynomial> of degree at most $n$, since its interpolant is itself. For a general continuous $f$, replacing $f$ by its interpolant supplies the approximation.

= 2
{parent=P}
