= a
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The first pivot is two. <Gaussian elimination> without <partial pivoting> uses $R_2\leftarrow R_2-2R_1$ and $R_3\leftarrow R_3-4R_1$, giving
$$
\begin{pmatrix}2&1&1\\0&1&1\\0&3&5\end{pmatrix}.
$$
The second pivot is one; $R_3\leftarrow R_3-3R_2$ gives the <upper triangular matrix>
$$
\boxed{U=\begin{pmatrix}2&1&1\\0&1&1\\0&0&2\end{pmatrix}.}
$$
Both pivots used for division are nonzero, so no row exchange is needed.

= b
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The elimination multipliers are $m_{21}=2$, $m_{31}=4$, and $m_{32}=3$. The unit <lower triangular matrix> in the <LU factorization> is therefore
$$
\boxed{L=\begin{pmatrix}1&0&0\\2&1&0\\4&3&1\end{pmatrix}.}
$$
Direct multiplication checks
$$
LU=\begin{pmatrix}2&1&1\\4&3&3\\8&7&9\end{pmatrix}=A.
$$
The positive signs in $L$ undo the row subtractions used during <Gaussian elimination>.

= c
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Take the <invertible matrix> $A=\left(\begin{smallmatrix}0&1\\1&0\end{smallmatrix}\right)$, whose determinant is $-1$. Suppose it had an <LU factorization> with any lower triangular $L$ and upper triangular $U$:
$$
L=\begin{pmatrix}\ell_{11}&0\\\ell_{21}&\ell_{22}\end{pmatrix},\qquad U=\begin{pmatrix}u_{11}&u_{12}\\0&u_{22}\end{pmatrix}.
$$
The $(1,2)$ entry gives $\ell_{11}u_{12}=1$, so $\ell_{11}\ne0$. The $(1,1)$ entry gives $\ell_{11}u_{11}=0$, so $u_{11}=0$. But then the $(2,1)$ entry would be $\ell_{21}u_{11}=0$, contradicting $a_{21}=1$. Thus **this nonsingular matrix has no unpermuted LU factorization**, even without imposing a unit diagonal on $L$. A row permutation resolves the zero pivot, explaining the role of <partial pivoting>.
